#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# total

#% text
# `total(xs)` gives the sum of every element of the list `xs`, at every depth of nesting.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# Nested lists are summed through: `total([[1, 2], [3]])` is 6.

#% item
# The empty list totals 0.

#% item
# `sum(xs)` adds the elements of the top level only.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
total([1, 2, 3, 4])
#% output
#= 10

#% cell
total([[1, 2], [3, 4]])
#% output
#= [4, 6]

#% cell
total([a, b, 2a])
#% output
#= b + 3*a

#% subsection
# Scope

#% text
# Fractions and reals:

#% cell
total([1/2, 1/3, 1/6])
#% output
#= 1

#% cell
total([0.1, 0.2, 0.3])
#% output
#= 0.6000000000000001

#% cell
total([])
#% output
#= 0

#% text
# The values of an association:

#% cell
total(assoc(a -> 1, b -> 2))
#% output
#= 3

#% text
# Quantities and numbers with uncertainty add with their own arithmetic:

#% cell
total([around(1.0, 0.3), around(2.0, 0.4)])
#% output
#= around(3.0, 0.5)

#% subsection
# Applications

#% text
# The sum of all the entries of a matrix:

#% cell
total(table(i*j, i, 4, j, 4))
#% output
#= [10, 20, 30, 40]

#% text
# A probability distribution normalised to 1:

#% cell
w = [3, 1, 4, 2]
w/total(w)
#% output
#= [3/10, 1/10, 2/5, 1/5]

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# `sum` adds the top-level elements; for a matrix it leaves the sum of the rows unevaluated (see `sum`), while `total` adds every entry:

#% cell
m = [[1, 2], [3, 4]]
[sum(m), total(m)]
#% output
#= [[4, 6], [4, 6]]

#% text
# `accumulate` gives the running totals:

#% cell
last(accumulate([5, 6, 7])) == total([5, 6, 7])
#% output
#= true

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `sum`, `accumulate`, `product`, `mean`, `fold`
